e  to do anything about it I do think we  can change the way we address skills and  we have to address it and it will be a  feature of an incoming labor government  that we will think strategically and in  the long term that's what I mean by  partnership a government being clear  about what we need to achieve the time  frame in which we will want to achieve  it and how we work in partnership with  business to achieve it but I'm certainly  Lizzy not going to give up on any of  this on the basis that this government's  failed to do it that's why we need the  change that's why we need a general  election  uh Graham from the mirror  please  good morning um Graham has got some  Daily Mirror energy is one of the big  concerns for some if not many people in  this room  um what would labor do to help those  companies and given the wider energy  crisis what would the party do to help  businesses and households that doesn't  mean yet higher government borrowing or  the need for deeper spending cuts again  every question this is good every  question is highlighting this problem of  short-term fixes versus long-time  Solutions now we need we need something  that answer the question how can you pay  your bills this winter we get that  that's why we LED best part of a year  ago now on the idea of  um you know ensuring that we use some of  those oil and gas profits the profits  they didn't expect to make and put it  towards our energy cost but that's not a  longer term or midterm answer we need to  run towards Renewables we need an  independent source of energy of our own  and we need to frankly knock out some of  the obstacles in the way if you take  onshore wind farms this government is  still blocking on Shore wind farms  probably the cheapest form of renewable  energy we could have and if we're going  to go down that road I'll be clear with  you lots of businesses have said to me  it's planning and regulation like that  that's holding us up you know I've had  Frank conversations with CEOs who say we  could build a wind farm in under two  years  but it takes seven years by the time you  add in the planning and the regulation  the role of government is to knock some  of that out of the way and so we need a  longer term solution to this whether  it's wind farms or anything else but  running towards renewable energy there  are so many opportunities for us but we  need to do it running towards that  challenge I'll give you another example  that I find deeply deeply frustrating 19  million homes in this country are  leaking their energy because they're too  drafty so it's costing a fortune to heat  a house which is then losing the heat  because the house is drafting ten years  ago we set about doing something about  that that's all dropped off in the last  few years I said over a year ago we  should have a national mission to  retrofit our houses to make sure they're  better insulated and nothing's been done  about it if we've done something about  it two million houses now would be  better insulated two million families be  paying less bills we've got to start  thinking about the medium and the long  term and working together in a partner  ship to achieve that because otherwise  we'll be doing what we've done for the  last 12 years it's a sticking plaster  sticking plaster sticking plaster never  address the real problem and that is  what leads in the end to stagnation the  single biggest problem in the last 12  years is our problem on growth thank you  very much and I think we've got Dan from  the ft  thank you  um can I ask you to be clear first of  all that you think immigration levels  currently are too high and that your  policies will reduce in a net uh  reduction in Immigration or simply lead  to different sorts of immigration and  secondly can I ask uh after a weekend of  reading about Swiss style trade deals  why a labor brexit deal with the EU  looks like  uh thank you Dan I mean on the question  of numbers  um I always start with what is driving  the numbers when we discuss immigration  and if what's driving the numbers  is our skills failure  or one of the big drivers is skills  failure then I think we need to address  the skills issue rather than just  talking about arbitrary numbers which is  why I've set out the argument I have in  the way I've made my speech this morning  and that does mean if we get this right  that immigration will go down in some of  those areas that are overly reliant on  immigration  but equally I'm not going to hold  businesses back if there's Innovation  Technologies where we do need  um you know Talent from abroad but you  know where we've been over Reliant and  where we've been over Reliant because  we've failed on skills I want to address  that by fixing the skills issue I want  to address the issue we've got with  hundreds of thousands of people out of  the labor market now when they were in  it only a few years ago that as I say  takes us into the question of what are  we doing about the NHS and mental health  which is holding many people back so  that's the way in which I see it on the  Swiss model look I've said  a number of occasions  um that we are not going back  um to the EU and that means not going  back into the single Market or the  Customs Union but we have to make brexit  work the deal this government has got us  is not working well it's holding  business back it's holding growth back  there are things that we need to do to  address it some of them we could start  straight away the protocol in Northern  Ireland needs a pragmatic answer and I  think I've talked to businesses in  Northern Ireland they have pragmatic  approaches to how we could deal with the  protocol in Northern Ireland we need to  reduce trade barriers by being clear  that we want high standards there isn't  a conflict there there's something we  can build on we need to share more when  it comes to Education and Research and  scientific research in particular I  think there's much more we can do in the  security space if you look particularly  at the response to the Ukraine crisis  and the way NATO and the EU have worked  together so I do think that we can have  a better brexit I think that we have to  make brexit work and I think what's  happened so far has been a government  that simply said the words get brexit  done and put in place a deal which I  think most people in this room would say  is not working well for business it's  not working well for exports is not  working well for growth and I want to  fight the next election on growth I want  to fight the next election on the  economy and I want an incoming labor  government to have the economy and  growth of the economy as the absolute  Focus for what we want to deliver on in  a five-year labor government thank you  so much  thank you very much Brian  we've got time for about three questions  from the audience but I think I would  exercise there cheers privileged by one  ahead of that if you get the keys of  dining Street tomorrow what will be top  of your list for driving growth uh our  clean power 2030 which is setting out a  plan for what we want to achieve  um and it the plan and How We Do It Go  together I can't I really need to stress  this to this audience yes an incoming  government sets the goal that is  legitimate in a democracy you win the  right to set the missions we should be  talking to you about what the missions  are before we get there by the way so  that you think they're achievable but  then what we have is a partnership  Arrangement on on the reason I'm using  the partnership example so much now is I  want to build that relationship that  partnership way of working so that if  and when we come into government we can  do it from day one on that first day  hopefully in Downing Street so that  we're saying to you how do we deliver  this together what do you need us to do  as a government what leave us as the  government Paul what does business do in  this there are different models an  incoming government can say We'll suck  it all up to the government nationalize  everything we're not going to do that  Lincoln upcoming government can say  um well we set the mission but the  market knows best so we'll just back off  not going to do that a true partnership  where we work together to achieve the  missions of the next Labor government  okay first question uh  is Emma here Emma just if you see who  you are who your company is and then the  brief question thank you  my question really will allude probably  to the CBI uh theme go for growth and  and  really  what I would like to ask  um if you become a prime minister what  would be the first thing you would like  to change to make this country more  competitive and what would be your  expectation from business to support you  in your objectives  um Emma it's a version of what I've just  said to Brad which is  um setting out our mission for growth  how we're going firstly one we want  growth I think that's really because  I've obviously want better public  services but the two go together so  growth has to be the single Focus have  to be clear about what we mean by growth  and this is what I said in the speech  there are different models of growth  you can say we want to grow and one way  to do it is to grow London and the  southeast very very quickly even more  along to the southeast provides a lot  already and then use that to  redistribute across the rest of the  country I don't want that model of  growth I think one of the things that  held us back is that we're not using all  the places all the talent across the  whole of the country so  um it's a plan for growth and absolutely  clear understanding and we're about what  we mean by growth because I want to see  growth in every place across the whole  of the United Kingdom and then a way of  working with you and others to deliver  on that the way of working is as  important as the ambition because the  ambition will never be realized 